      <header class="page-header">
        <p class="page-kicker">Factions</p>
        <h1>The Flaying Courts</h1>
        
      </header>
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><div class="toc-title">On this page</div><div class="toc-links"><a href="#court-of-agony">Court of Agony</a><a href="#court-of-loss">Court of Loss</a><a href="#court-of-bells">Court of Bells</a><a href="#court-of-lightless">Court of Lightless</a><a href="#court-of-instance">Court of Instance</a><a href="#the-blood-empress">The Blood Empress</a></div></nav><article class="lore-page"><p>The Flaying Courts are fox daemons ruled by a Greater Goddess known only as the Blood Empress, who lives for, and loves only, herself and inflicting pain. Each court of the Flaying Courts worship different aspects of pain. Cruelty is their sustenance, pain is their delight, and skin is what they reap.</p>
<h2 id="court-of-agony">Court of Agony</h2>
<p>The Court of Agony, built around pain of the physical body, is within the First Open Sky. The ground, covered in green grass, is seemingly made of jagged glass in the shape and coloration of glass. The tall grass is shaped so to cut and knick at various arteries and muscles, designed to make you add yet another red trail to the countless other red trails within the First Open Sky.</p>
<p>It is best to be quick, however, for no traveller is alone. Wandering this sky are black-as-pitch humanoid figures, once people, long since disfigured and transformed into something entirely different. Their skin has vanished, replaced with a thick black flexible glass with large, long, jagged blades of glass jutting out from various places. Their only goal is to find a traveller, embrace them, and drag them towards the floating dens. The sludge they inject through their glass blades will keep any unfortunate enough to be embraced alive.</p>
<p>Inside the floating dens roam crude, fox-like daemons, clothed in fine drapes of skin. There, the travellers shall have their skin slowly stripped, their muscles liquified, and forced to remain living whilst the flexible black glass is poured onto them, all in a prayer of degenerate pleasure to the Empress of Blood.</p>
<h2 id="court-of-loss">Court of Loss</h2>
<p>The Court of Loss, within the Second Open Sky underneath the First, may only be reached upon finding the Well of Descension, a well ringed by unknown and countless limbs, grafted into the insides.</p>
<p>Upon descending the well, a new sky can be found high above. The sky, dripping with a sick yellow and covered with a blanket of black cloud, is broken only by the giant pillars of limbs of creatures known and unknown, reaching from the heavens to seemingly endless voids in the earth itself. The rivers and lakes cannot be trusted, for it might be water, but it might be corpse-water. The earth may be solid one moment, but the instant a traveller’s guard is down, the earth shall open to consume them.</p>
<p>Here roams the Daemons of the Mind. They crawl and skitter along, oddly small and snake-like, yet still fox-like, until they wrap around the traveler and attempt to dig into their skull, implanting visions of suffering, of loved ones dying, and of utter despair, purely for their own amusement.</p>
<h2 id="court-of-bells">Court of Bells</h2>
<p>The Court of Bells is located within the Third Open Sky, the entrance of which is the Well of Jingling. The Well of Jingling is located within the middle of a shallow puddle-lake of corpse-water. Instead of a proper well, the Second Well is a giant hole in the earth, ringed with endless chains, with the air below obscured by a gray mist.</p>
<p>The land beneath is plain and barren, an obscene flatness of ink black sand, cool to the touch with the moisture in the air. Hanging from the heavens here are chains larger than most towers in width, vanishing high above into the mist, darkened by giant rings that become visible, ringing bells the size of small towns, loud enough to shatter the hearing of anything nearby.</p>
<p>In long procession lines, vibrant blue four legged fox daemons, each standing on their hind four legs, walk. They are adorned in the skins of the fallen and slain, carrying tall black staffs lined with ringing bells atop the top end. They will capture any and all they come across, be they creature, man, or fellow daemon, and work at destroying their senses of hearing.</p>
<p>The Blood Empress appreciates the prayers of pleasure that the Court of Bells devotes to her, but finds disdain in their lack of creativity and seeming disconnect to the pleasure of it all.</p>
<h2 id="court-of-lightless">Court of Lightless</h2>
<p>The entrance to the home of the Court of Lightless, the Fourth Open Sky, is a white oak pillar streaming with blood. Ringed around this pillar is an emptiness, a void, where one must climb down the pillar to see what awaits them. This is the Well of Vision.</p>
<p>The skies are a twirling mass and mess of reds and blues and greens and yellows, forming an endless number of eyes, forever watching with baleful beams of hued light and pain cast from each unblinking eye. The ground twists and heaves violently, yet it is perfectly flat. It bends, but remains narrow and straight. It moves, yet it remains perfectly still. It causes pain just to watch it.</p>
<p>Daemons of this realm seek to capture those unfortunate enough to make it that far, dragging the screaming fools deeper and deeper into the realm. Pillars, made of exotic stones that descend into corpses and bodies either absorbed or carved into the pillars and adorned with great white spikes, are the destination of these captures. Once there, the fox daemons will impale their victims on these spikes, facing outwards and up into the violent hued gazes of the eyes high above. The spikes, made only to contain the victim, keeps them perpetually alive, though it does not prevent their victims from rotting.</p>
<p>Far below, ringed about these pillars, dance the daemons - for their joy is great, and all shall see the source of their pleasure.</p>
<h2 id="court-of-instance">Court of Instance</h2>
<p>The Fifth Open Sky, accessible by the Well of Ascension, is accessed by a valley-sized pit hidden deep within the Fourth Open Sky.</p>
<p>Far below, color ceases to exist. Warmth is a pleasantry of the past, coolness is a long-forgotten desire, and mere comfort has died long ago. Between odd slabs of white stone are embedded white corpses. Some deformed, others not, some with missing fingers, some with their skulls sliced in two, others impaled, and so on. There is no roof, reaching only endlessly and out of sight into an unfathomable black void, high above. Those who somehow make it to the Fifth Open Sky will eventually realize that the ground is not made of stone, but of flesh and bone and sinew, the same white of their surroundings.</p>
<p>All is dim, yet one will eventually adapt. But they are not alone.</p>
<p>The Kurogitsune, the fox daemons who call the Fifth Open Sky home, are the favored children of the Empress of Blood. Each footfall upon the fleshy ground is a calling to them. Slowly, they stalk and hulk along, tall and gaunt. Standing on their hind legs, their black fur is a stark contrast to their surroundings, though it’s still difficult to perceive them due to the general dimness of the Fifth Open Sky. Their many arms each wield various skeletal, jagged blades, each personalized for the Kurogitsune’s personal enjoyment and preference.</p>
<p>Upon making contact with the foul intruders, the Kurogitsune behead, impale, or eviscerate their prey with glee. The fallen, now abandoned even by the Kurogitsune in search of more prey, will slowly rot and fuse into the ground itself.</p>
<h2 id="the-blood-empress">The Blood Empress</h2>
<p>Long ago, in the Age of Divinity, there was a meagre goddess, a deity of small note, the local deity of spite. No matter what she did, no matter how she accomplished tasks of note, no matter how many other pantheons she helped topple, she was overshadowed by another goddess, otherwise known as the Lady of Pride.</p>
<p>At first, they had been twins, and the best of friends, but being overshadowed had begun to show the cost. Jealousy writhed through her being, and the little goddess began to plot and to scheme. First, she allied herself with the God of Pain. Then, the gods and goddesses of resentment, joy, catharsis, and of twisting came to be her allies - some willingly, some forced, others negotiated with. In secret she worked, gaining power as steadily as she could, becoming more degenerate and spiteful, more hateful and cruel.</p>
<p>Then, once again in secret, she began to consume her allies. First pain. Then joy, then resentment, then twisting, and finally catharsis. Each secret murder gave her power beyond measure, and yet she did not show her hand to the Lady of Pride. Not yet.</p>
<p>After the Lady of Pride and her inner circle conquered another realm, this meagre goddess invited the Lady of Pride and her inner circle to dine and feast in a great banquet, hosted in the honor of the Lady of Pride. Gleefully, the goddess accepted, thinking none of it - after all, this was her rival, her sister, her best of friends, her closest confidant.</p>
<p>The meagre goddess poisoned the inner circle, cursing them to die with their food and their drink, and poisoned the Lady of Pride with a powerful concoction that removed the will of her limbs and her voice. With great glee, the meagre goddess exposed her plan, and began to work her art of pains and joys upon the Lady of Pride, taking a grim catharsis in doing so. When the deed was done, the Lady of Pride’s soul was imbued within her own skull, which was then detached and re-fashioned into a chalice.</p>
<p>Thus began the First Age of Madness, marking the birth of the Empress of Blood, the kinslayer.</p>
<p>From her will, she created the Kurogitsune, freakish fox-like monstrosities and daemons that served as her children, her most precious designs. She traveled the lands round about, turning all who crossed her into trinkets and trophies, keeping them alive within, eternally showing her superiority over they.</p>
<p>She settled in the Darklands, deeming it to be the most pleasant and beautiful place, before twisting it and establishing her Great Courts, eventually coming to be known as The Flaying Courts. None approach, lest they become the subject of the Empress of Blood’s degenerate and filthy pleasure and become living pieces of pained art.</p></article>
